{"level":30,"time":1776970186341,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SXGNbza6stPwfpauZBEcDK","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# City Arborist Review\n     2→\n     3→*Specifies tree preservation criteria, variance requirements for heritage trees, and mitigation procedures.*\n     4→\n     5→City Arborist Review – Alexander DeRoehn (512) 974-6597 - alexander.deroehn@austintexas.gov\n     6→\n     7→Update 0 6/13/25\n     8→Update 1 9/26/25\n     9→Note: Please e-mail me if you have any questions, concerns, or require additional information about these comments: Alexander.Deroehn@austintexas.gov. You may also e-mail me if you would like to schedule a phone or virtual meeting to clarify the review comments. Updates cannot be reviewed outside of the update review cycle.\n    10→\n    11→CA1 – Current Status: Pending\n    12→* U1: Remove plantings from the half CRZ of trees to remain.\n    13→* U0: Ensure all trees meet the following tree preservation criteria: \n    14→  1. At least fifty percent of the total area (square footage) of the Critical Root Zone must be preserved at natural grade, with natural ground cover. \n    15→  2. The entirety of the Half CRZ must be protected, with the exception that cut or fill of 4\" or less is allowed within the Half CRZ. \n    16→  3. No cut or fill is allowed within the Quarter CRZ. \n    17→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.1\n    18→\n    19→[Figure: Series of four circular diagrams illustrating allowable impacts to the Critical Root Zone (CRZ) of a tree, showing 30%, 60%, 50%, and 75% impacts respectively]\n    20→\n    21→CA2 – Current Status: Pending\n    22→* U1: Not addressed. A heritage American elm is shown to be removed on landscape sheets. \n    23→* U0: Administrative Variance\n    24→An administrative variance may be granted for a heritage tree that does not have at least one stem that is 30 inches in diameter or larger to be removed only after determining by the City Arborist that the tree is dead, diseased, or an imminent hazard. If not dead, diseased or an imminent hazard, clearly show that the tree prevents reasonable use or access of the property and that all design options have been exhausted.\n    25→Reference: LDC Section 25-8-642\n    26→\n    27→The following items are required to review an administrative variance request to remove a Heritage tree:\n    28→1. Please provide a variance request memo on letterhead (any letterhead works) that states tree id, species and DBH of heritage tree(s) proposed for removal. Reference LDC 25-8-642 to prepare the memo.\n    29→2. Letter shall include applicant findings per the LDC and provide alternative layouts demonstrating preservation is not feasible. \n    30→3. Please pay the administrative variance fee. This fee is added once the memo has been received. \n    31→4. If removal is condition related, provide a third-party arborist report with clear photos of the tree’s condition. Please provide a ISA basic tree risk assessment report if trying to claim DDI status. \n    32→5. If a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision would eliminate the need for a variance from LDC Section 25-8-641 before requesting a variance to allow removal of a heritage tree. Please demonstrate that a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision that would eliminate the need for a variance has been denied. \n    33→\n    34→CA3 – Current Status: Pending\n    35→* U1: Not addressed. Show this on all sheets including demolition erosion and site plan sheets. \n    36→* U0: Graphically show the Half critical root zone of all trees located adjacent to proposed demolition and construction for review. \n    37→\n    38→CA4 – Current Status: Pending\n    39→* U1: Not addressed. Tree fencing is missing from the following trees: Entire east property line, 4038 and 4039, 4018 and 4019. Fencing is shown for tree 4045 despite this being shown as removed. \n    40→* U0: Expand tree fencing to the furthest extent possible for each tree, ensuring compliance with CA1. Tree fencing cannot be placed over existing, or proposed hardscape, grading etc. Provide fencing for all trees with a CRZ extending into the LOC. Show fencing on erosion control and demolition sheets. \n    41→\n    42→CA5 – Current Status: Pending\n    43→* U1: Silt fence is still shown within the CRZ of trees to be retained. \n    44→* U0: ESC Plan\n    45→Where it is to be installed within the critical root zones of regulated size trees, within and adjacent to the limits of construction, remove silt fence and replace with mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems (silt fence requires 6” of cut for installation). Show the mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems with a symbol in place of a silt fencing symbol. \n    46→Erosion and sediment controls should be placed outside of the tree protection fencing so that fencing will not need to be moved to maintain the controls.\n    47→Reference: ECM 3.5.2\n    48→\n    49→CA6 – Current Status: Pending\n    50→* U1: Not addressed. Tree 4022 critical root zones on the erosion control plan are still not shown to scale.. \n    51→* U0: Trees are to be represented on plans by a circle centered on the trunk location, where the radius of the circle is a number of feet equal to the diameter in inches of the tree. Tree CRZs appear to be incorrect size based on the provided survey. \n    52→Reference: ECM 3.3.2.D.2\n    53→\n    54→CA7 – Current Status: Cleared\n    55→* U0: Provide a tree list on sheet 8 listing all trees, sizes species and labeling which trees are to be removed. \n    56→\n    57→CA8 – Current Status: Pending\n    58→* U1: New impacts and construction entrance within the CRZ of trees 4038 and 4039 which appear to exceed 50% allowable impacts (See CA1). Further, new impacts are proposed within the CRZ of trees along the east property line Demonstrate compliance with CA1 and criteria below. \n    59→* U0: Existing Impacts & Meeting 50%\n    60→Proposed impacts in the Half or Quarter CRZ are allowed within the footprint of existing impacts if they do not exceed the depth or width of those impacts. Additional depth of impact may be allowed if the applicant demonstrates the absence of significant roots. \n    61→To determine whether roots are likely to have extended under an existing impact, please contact a private arborist to air-excavate in the critical root zone adjacent to the edge of the impact (slab foundation, asphalt drive, etc)The arborist needs to air-excavate a 1ft width along the entire span of the critical root zone that has been impacted. \n    62→The air-excavation should be at a 24 inch depth. \n    63→The arborist needs to take pictures that clearly show whether or not roots exist along the area of the trench, extending into the impacted area.\n    64→Please provide pictures showing the root system prior to it being backfilled with soil. Ensure that the pictures are well-lit, and the view is not obstructed.\n    65→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.B.4.a\n    66→\n    67→CA9 – Current Status: Pending\n    68→* U1: comment pending\n    69→* U0: Trees 4034, 4036, 4045, 4026, 4014, 4009\n    70→Excessive pruning constitutes removal of a regulated tree per Land Development Code 25-8-603. Pruning shall conform to the most recent ANSI A300 pruning standard. Not more than twenty-five percent of the foliage shall be removed within an annual growing season. Failure to account for the particular branch structure of any regulated tree may result in a plan revision or project delay if approved plans require pruning that would not comply with crown preservation requirements. \n    71→Demonstrate how the proposed construction (e.g. building, grading, detention pond, etc) including the installation of scaffolding, does not require removal of more than 25% of the crown of the preserved trees. Provide a tree crown assessment by a qualified 3rd party arborist that studies the overall proposed impacts to the crown, identifies and shows the exact location of major pruning cuts, and estimates the overall crown percentage to be removed.\n    72→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.2\n    73→\n    74→CA10 – Current Status: Pending\n    75→* U1: comment pending\n    76→* U0: Grading\n    77→Modify the proposed grading to meet preservation criteria.\n    78→A grade change of a few inches can be detrimental to a tree, yet most sites require extensive cut and fill in order to manage drainage flow. Some design alternatives which can be used to preserve significant trees are as follows:\n    79→  o Use berms or retaining walls instead of cutting to provide detention.\n    80→  o Provide tree well and/or aeration systems for trees in fill areas (see City of Austin Standard Specifications and City of Austin Standards).\n    81→  o Provide retaining walls to mitigate cuts and fills (see Figure 3-10 in Appendix V of this manual).\n    82→Reference: ECM 3.5.2 .A.3\n    83→\n    84→CA11 – Current Status: Pending\n    85→* U1: comment pending\n    86→* U0: Please demonstrate what the required over-build(s) will be for the proposed building types. This information is necessary to determine where the real line-of-impact will be in relation to the CRZs of trees to be preserved.\n    87→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.3\n    88→\n    89→CA12 – Current Status: Pending\n    90→* U1: comment pending\n    91→* U0: Provide a retaining wall detail showing the required over excavation near preserved trees. Ensure that retaining walls, footers and all over excavation is fully outside of the half CRZ. \n    92→\n    93→CA13 – Current Status: Cleared\n    94→* U0: Clarify if wood fence on the east side of the site is existing or proposed. If proposed, provide the following: \n    95→* Relocate entirely outside of the quarter CRZ. \n    96→* For any installation in the Half CRZ \n    97→  o A call out is needed in the corresponding CRZ(s) for: \n    98→    ▪ post holes to be dug by hand or air excavated\n    99→    ▪ post holes shifted to avoid 1.5” diameter or greater roots\n   100→    ▪ a vapor barrier installed between any poured concrete and the soil. \n   101→* Provide a detail in the plan set for the fence posts and footings to be installed near trees.\n   102→\n   103→CA14 – Current Status: Pending\n   104→* U1: There appears to be open areas for planting. Additionally, mitigation trees are shown spaced 20 feet apart. This would likely allow for denser planting. \n   105→* U0: Of the 249.50 mitigation inches owed, only 40 are proposed. Fee in lieu is considered a secondary option to planting. Demonstrate that all plantable space has been utilized before a fee in lieu is discussed. \n   106→\n   107→CA15 – Current Status: Cleared\n   108→* U0: Ensure tree list meets the following\n   109→* Qualifying Species - Significant Shade Providing Trees\n   110→No less than 75% of the total caliper inches of mitigation trees required must be represented by significant shade trees located in the ECM Appendix F. The remaining 25% of total caliper inches may be represented by a mixture of the ECM Appendix F tree species.\n   111→All trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities). Eligible replacement tree species are listed in Environmental Criteria Manual Appendix F. The City Arborist may approve species not listed in Appendix F for mitigation credit.\n   112→Reference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b \n   113→* Qualifying Species - Diversity\n   114→  1. When twenty or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least two species.\n   115→  2. When one hundred or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least five species, with no single species representing more than fifty percent of the total caliper of the required mitigation.\n   116→All trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities).\n   117→Reference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b\n   118→\n   119→CA16 – Current Status: Pending\n   120→* U1: Comment to remain as reference. Update table with any subsequent plan changes. \n   121→* U0: Once all tree preservation comments have been cleared, please provide the following information on the plan for urban forest accounting purposes:\n   122→\n   123→Surveyed:\n   124→* Total Appendix F tree inches surveyed;\n   125→* Heritage tree inches surveyed;\n   126→* Non-Appendix F tree inches surveyed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n   127→* Invasive tree inches surveyed;\n   128→\n   129→Removed:\n   130→* Total Appendix F inches removed;\n   131→* Heritage Tree inches removed;\n   132→* Total Non-Appendix F inches removed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n   133→* Invasive inches removed;\n   134→* Total Dead, Diseased, or Imminent Hazard (DDI) inches removed;\n   135→* DDI Appendix F inches removed;\n   136→* DDI Heritage tree inches removed;\n   137→* DDI Non-Appendix F inches removed;\n   138→* DDI Invasive inches removed;\n   139→\n   140→Mitigation:\n   141→* Total mitigation replacement inches planted;\n   142→* Total replacement inches planted on site (private trees);\n   143→* Total replacement ROW inches planted;\n   144→* Private inches owed to Urban Forest Replenishment Fund (UFRF) \n   145→* Public inches owed to UFRF \n   146→* Total non-mitigation inches planted on site; [ECM 3.5.4]\n   147→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2af985ee-646f-4363-9e8d-5974ccc4a371","uuid":"eb93a64d-4f69-496f-a78a-16df18aec4d1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/14-city-arborist-review.md","content":"# City Arborist Review\n\n*Specifies tree preservation criteria, variance requirements for heritage trees, and mitigation procedures.*\n\nCity Arborist Review – Alexander DeRoehn (512) 974-6597 - alexander.deroehn@austintexas.gov\n\nUpdate 0 6/13/25\nUpdate 1 9/26/25\nNote: Please e-mail me if you have any questions, concerns, or require additional information about these comments: Alexander.Deroehn@austintexas.gov. You may also e-mail me if you would like to schedule a phone or virtual meeting to clarify the review comments. Updates cannot be reviewed outside of the update review cycle.\n\nCA1 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Remove plantings from the half CRZ of trees to remain.\n* U0: Ensure all trees meet the following tree preservation criteria: \n  1. At least fifty percent of the total area (square footage) of the Critical Root Zone must be preserved at natural grade, with natural ground cover. \n  2. The entirety of the Half CRZ must be protected, with the exception that cut or fill of 4\" or less is allowed within the Half CRZ. \n  3. No cut or fill is allowed within the Quarter CRZ. \nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.1\n\n[Figure: Series of four circular diagrams illustrating allowable impacts to the Critical Root Zone (CRZ) of a tree, showing 30%, 60%, 50%, and 75% impacts respectively]\n\nCA2 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. A heritage American elm is shown to be removed on landscape sheets. \n* U0: Administrative Variance\nAn administrative variance may be granted for a heritage tree that does not have at least one stem that is 30 inches in diameter or larger to be removed only after determining by the City Arborist that the tree is dead, diseased, or an imminent hazard. If not dead, diseased or an imminent hazard, clearly show that the tree prevents reasonable use or access of the property and that all design options have been exhausted.\nReference: LDC Section 25-8-642\n\nThe following items are required to review an administrative variance request to remove a Heritage tree:\n1. Please provide a variance request memo on letterhead (any letterhead works) that states tree id, species and DBH of heritage tree(s) proposed for removal. Reference LDC 25-8-642 to prepare the memo.\n2. Letter shall include applicant findings per the LDC and provide alternative layouts demonstrating preservation is not feasible. \n3. Please pay the administrative variance fee. This fee is added once the memo has been received. \n4. If removal is condition related, provide a third-party arborist report with clear photos of the tree’s condition. Please provide a ISA basic tree risk assessment report if trying to claim DDI status. \n5. If a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision would eliminate the need for a variance from LDC Section 25-8-641 before requesting a variance to allow removal of a heritage tree. Please demonstrate that a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision that would eliminate the need for a variance has been denied. \n\nCA3 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Show this on all sheets including demolition erosion and site plan sheets. \n* U0: Graphically show the Half critical root zone of all trees located adjacent to proposed demolition and construction for review. \n\nCA4 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Tree fencing is missing from the following trees: Entire east property line, 4038 and 4039, 4018 and 4019. Fencing is shown for tree 4045 despite this being shown as removed. \n* U0: Expand tree fencing to the furthest extent possible for each tree, ensuring compliance with CA1. Tree fencing cannot be placed over existing, or proposed hardscape, grading etc. Provide fencing for all trees with a CRZ extending into the LOC. Show fencing on erosion control and demolition sheets. \n\nCA5 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Silt fence is still shown within the CRZ of trees to be retained. \n* U0: ESC Plan\nWhere it is to be installed within the critical root zones of regulated size trees, within and adjacent to the limits of construction, remove silt fence and replace with mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems (silt fence requires 6” of cut for installation). Show the mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems with a symbol in place of a silt fencing symbol. \nErosion and sediment controls should be placed outside of the tree protection fencing so that fencing will not need to be moved to maintain the controls.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2\n\nCA6 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Tree 4022 critical root zones on the erosion control plan are still not shown to scale.. \n* U0: Trees are to be represented on plans by a circle centered on the trunk location, where the radius of the circle is a number of feet equal to the diameter in inches of the tree. Tree CRZs appear to be incorrect size based on the provided survey. \nReference: ECM 3.3.2.D.2\n\nCA7 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Provide a tree list on sheet 8 listing all trees, sizes species and labeling which trees are to be removed. \n\nCA8 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: New impacts and construction entrance within the CRZ of trees 4038 and 4039 which appear to exceed 50% allowable impacts (See CA1). Further, new impacts are proposed within the CRZ of trees along the east property line Demonstrate compliance with CA1 and criteria below. \n* U0: Existing Impacts & Meeting 50%\nProposed impacts in the Half or Quarter CRZ are allowed within the footprint of existing impacts if they do not exceed the depth or width of those impacts. Additional depth of impact may be allowed if the applicant demonstrates the absence of significant roots. \nTo determine whether roots are likely to have extended under an existing impact, please contact a private arborist to air-excavate in the critical root zone adjacent to the edge of the impact (slab foundation, asphalt drive, etc)The arborist needs to air-excavate a 1ft width along the entire span of the critical root zone that has been impacted. \nThe air-excavation should be at a 24 inch depth. \nThe arborist needs to take pictures that clearly show whether or not roots exist along the area of the trench, extending into the impacted area.\nPlease provide pictures showing the root system prior to it being backfilled with soil. Ensure that the pictures are well-lit, and the view is not obstructed.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.B.4.a\n\nCA9 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Trees 4034, 4036, 4045, 4026, 4014, 4009\nExcessive pruning constitutes removal of a regulated tree per Land Development Code 25-8-603. Pruning shall conform to the most recent ANSI A300 pruning standard. Not more than twenty-five percent of the foliage shall be removed within an annual growing season. Failure to account for the particular branch structure of any regulated tree may result in a plan revision or project delay if approved plans require pruning that would not comply with crown preservation requirements. \nDemonstrate how the proposed construction (e.g. building, grading, detention pond, etc) including the installation of scaffolding, does not require removal of more than 25% of the crown of the preserved trees. Provide a tree crown assessment by a qualified 3rd party arborist that studies the overall proposed impacts to the crown, identifies and shows the exact location of major pruning cuts, and estimates the overall crown percentage to be removed.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.2\n\nCA10 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Grading\nModify the proposed grading to meet preservation criteria.\nA grade change of a few inches can be detrimental to a tree, yet most sites require extensive cut and fill in order to manage drainage flow. Some design alternatives which can be used to preserve significant trees are as follows:\n  o Use berms or retaining walls instead of cutting to provide detention.\n  o Provide tree well and/or aeration systems for trees in fill areas (see City of Austin Standard Specifications and City of Austin Standards).\n  o Provide retaining walls to mitigate cuts and fills (see Figure 3-10 in Appendix V of this manual).\nReference: ECM 3.5.2 .A.3\n\nCA11 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Please demonstrate what the required over-build(s) will be for the proposed building types. This information is necessary to determine where the real line-of-impact will be in relation to the CRZs of trees to be preserved.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.3\n\nCA12 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Provide a retaining wall detail showing the required over excavation near preserved trees. Ensure that retaining walls, footers and all over excavation is fully outside of the half CRZ. \n\nCA13 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Clarify if wood fence on the east side of the site is existing or proposed. If proposed, provide the following: \n* Relocate entirely outside of the quarter CRZ. \n* For any installation in the Half CRZ \n  o A call out is needed in the corresponding CRZ(s) for: \n    ▪ post holes to be dug by hand or air excavated\n    ▪ post holes shifted to avoid 1.5” diameter or greater roots\n    ▪ a vapor barrier installed between any poured concrete and the soil. \n* Provide a detail in the plan set for the fence posts and footings to be installed near trees.\n\nCA14 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: There appears to be open areas for planting. Additionally, mitigation trees are shown spaced 20 feet apart. This would likely allow for denser planting. \n* U0: Of the 249.50 mitigation inches owed, only 40 are proposed. Fee in lieu is considered a secondary option to planting. Demonstrate that all plantable space has been utilized before a fee in lieu is discussed. \n\nCA15 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Ensure tree list meets the following\n* Qualifying Species - Significant Shade Providing Trees\nNo less than 75% of the total caliper inches of mitigation trees required must be represented by significant shade trees located in the ECM Appendix F. The remaining 25% of total caliper inches may be represented by a mixture of the ECM Appendix F tree species.\nAll trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities). Eligible replacement tree species are listed in Environmental Criteria Manual Appendix F. The City Arborist may approve species not listed in Appendix F for mitigation credit.\nReference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b \n* Qualifying Species - Diversity\n  1. When twenty or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least two species.\n  2. When one hundred or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least five species, with no single species representing more than fifty percent of the total caliper of the required mitigation.\nAll trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities).\nReference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b\n\nCA16 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Comment to remain as reference. Update table with any subsequent plan changes. \n* U0: Once all tree preservation comments have been cleared, please provide the following information on the plan for urban forest accounting purposes:\n\nSurveyed:\n* Total Appendix F tree inches surveyed;\n* Heritage tree inches surveyed;\n* Non-Appendix F tree inches surveyed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n* Invasive tree inches surveyed;\n\nRemoved:\n* Total Appendix F inches removed;\n* Heritage Tree inches removed;\n* Total Non-Appendix F inches removed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n* Invasive inches removed;\n* Total Dead, Diseased, or Imminent Hazard (DDI) inches removed;\n* DDI Appendix F inches removed;\n* DDI Heritage tree inches removed;\n* DDI Non-Appendix F inches removed;\n* DDI Invasive inches removed;\n\nMitigation:\n* Total mitigation replacement inches planted;\n* Total replacement inches planted on site (private trees);\n* Total replacement ROW inches planted;\n* Private inches owed to Urban Forest Replenishment Fund (UFRF) \n* Public inches owed to UFRF \n* Total non-mitigation inches planted on site; [ECM 3.5.4]\n","numLines":147,"startLine":1,"totalLines":147}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776970186341,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NkxC7y7agde5kNkTj9xHAt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The City Arborist review document discusses tree preservation and mitigation but doesn't specifically address the overhead electric clearances (which is an Austin Energy concern, not arborist concern).\n\nHowever, I notice CA14 mentions \"mitigation trees are shown spaced 20 feet apart\" - this is important information that confirms the 5 mitigation trees along the southern boundary are intentional plantings, not existing trees.\n\nGiven the information I've gathered:\n\n1. **5 mitigation trees (\"M\" labeled) are located 5-10 feet from the OHE line** on the southern boundary\n2. **These are proposed NEW plantings** (not existing trees)\n3. **The specific species cannot be determined from Sheet 31 alone** - must reference Sheet 35 plant schedule\n4. **From Sheet 35, the proposed mitigation trees include:**\n   - Live Oak (Quercus virginiana) - very large tree (40-60+ ft)\n   - Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) - very large tree (40-50 ft)\n   - Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora) - small tree (15-25 ft)\n   - Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana) - medium tree (20-30 ft)\n\n5. **Professional knowledge about these species and utility compatibility:**\n   - Live Oak: Large tree, NOT utility-compatible (problematic for utilities)\n   - Cedar Elm: Large tree, NOT utility-compatible (similar to American Elm)\n   - Texas Mountain Laurel: Smaller tree, LIKELY utility-compatible\n   - Mexican Redbud: Medium tree, LIKELY utility-compatible\n\nNow I need to determine which checklist items FAIL based on this evidence:\n\n**EL-2.1:** \"Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment\"\n- Trees are 5-10 feet from OHE line (within 15-foot zone)\n\n\n- Live Oak and Cedar Elm are both problematic species for utilities, and if either appears in the 5 mitigation trees, this criterion fails. Without the legend showing exact species placement, I can't rule this out.\n\n**EL-2.3:** \"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment\"\n- The 5-10 foot proximity is well within the 25-foot restriction zone. Since Live Oak and Cedar Elm both exceed 40 feet at maturity and are among the proposed species, there's a strong likelihood this fails unless the plan specifically excludes these larger varieties from the mitigation planting.\n\n**EL-2.14:** \"Trees not shown on landscape plan in relation to overhead electric facilities, preventing clearance verification\"\n- The trees and OHE lines are both depicted on the plan, so the spatial relationship is visible. However, species identification relies on cross-referencing Sheet 35 rather than being labeled directly on the landscape plan itself. This creates ambiguity about whether the requirement is satisfied or if it's actually a deficiency under EL-2.10.\n\n**EL-2.10:** \"Plant legend or plant schedule does not identify species of trees proposed near electric facilities\"\n- The species identification appears incomplete on the primary landscape plan, which could constitute a failure depending on how strictly the code interprets \"near electric facilities.\"\n\n**EL-2.12 and EL-2.13:** Both address dimensioned clearances, which aren't shown on the plan at all, making these items not applicable.\n\nThe key issues emerging are the lack of direct species labeling on the landscape plan itself and the absence of any dimensioned clearance measurements. Large trees positioned near the OHE line appear to be the primary concern. Flag EL-2.1, EL-2.3, and EL-2.10 as problematic since I can't definitively confirm species identification from the landscape plan alone. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 might clarify which trees are positioned where, so I should review that more carefully before finalizing my assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3930,"cache_read_input_tokens":69178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3930},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2af985ee-646f-4363-9e8d-5974ccc4a371","uuid":"d29e8397-50a5-4feb-abed-ae85264fbd2a"}
{"level":30,"time":1776970186342,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NkxC7y7agde5kNkTj9xHAt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my research, I can now assess the checklist items. Let me summarize my findings:\n\n**Research Summary - NOT FOUND / COULD NOT VERIFY:**\n1. Specific species identification for the 5 mitigation trees (\"M\" labeled) shown 5-10 feet from OHE line on Sheet 31 - requires cross-referencing to Sheet 35 planting legend which is not clearly linked on landscape plan\n2. Utility-compatible (UC) status designation per ECM Appendix F for proposed trees - not provided in plan set\n3. Mature height classifications for proposed species per ECM Appendix F - only minimum container heights provided (16', 8', 10' planting heights), not mature heights\n4. Whether any trees are transmission or distribution facility (voltage level not specified on plan)\n\n**Key Evidence Found:**\n- Sheet 31 shows 5 proposed mitigation trees (\"M\") plotted 5-10 feet from the OHE line along southern boundary\n- Sheet 35 plant schedule lists: 10 Cedar Elm, 2 Live Oak, 6 Mexican Redbud, 2 Texas Mountain Laurel\n- These are large native trees commonly growing to 40-50+ feet mature height (Cedar Elm, Live Oak)\n- Trees are clearly depicted ON the landscape plan relative to OHE, but specific species identification on Sheet 31 itself is incomplete\n- No clearance dimensions are provided on any sheets\n\nNow let me prepare the structured findings for the 15 checklist items:\n\n1. **EL-2.1** (Non-utility-compatible trees within 15 ft of distribution): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Trees shown at 5-10 ft from OHE, but specific species cannot be determined from landscape plan alone\n2. **EL-2.2** (Non-utility-compatible trees within 50 ft of transmission): **PASS** - No transmission facilities indicated; standard distribution (pole spacing and infrastructure match distribution configuration)\n3. **EL-2.3** (Large trees within 25 ft of distribution): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Trees at 5-10 ft (within clearance zone), but cannot confirm all are ≥40 ft mature height without ECM Appendix F data\n4. **EL-2.4** (Large trees within 50 ft of transmission): **PASS** - No transmission identified\n5. **EL-2.5** (Trees in transmission easement): **PASS** - No transmission easement indicated\n6. **EL-2.6** (Trees within 10 ft of poles): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Utility pole locations not specifically marked on landscape plan\n7. **EL-2.7** (Trees within 10 ft of pad-mounted equipment): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Pad-mount locations from electrical plan (Sheet 21) not cross-referenced on landscape plan (Sheet 31)\n8. **EL-2.8** (Trees within 5 ft of underground equipment): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Underground equipment locations not shown on landscape plan\n9. **EL-2.9** (Root barriers for trees within 20 ft of underground equipment): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Cannot determine if trees are within 20 ft of underground equipment without equipment locations on landscape plan\n10. **EL-2.10** (Plant legend/schedule does not identify species near electric facilities): **FAIL** - Mitigation trees near OHE are labeled only as \"M\" with symbols; specific species must be cross-referenced to Sheet 35\n11. **EL-2.11** (Large trees not differentiated on plans): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Cannot determine which of the proposed trees are large (≥40 ft) from landscape plan alone\n12. **EL-2.12** (Clearances measured from pole instead of conductor): **PASS** - No clearances dimensioned on plans, so measurement methodology not applicable\n13. **EL-2.13** (Clearances measured from canopy instead of trunk): **PASS** - No clearances dimensioned on plans\n14. **EL-2.14** (Trees not shown on landscape plan relative to OHE): **PASS** - Trees and OHE both clearly depicted on Sheet 31\n15. **EL-2.15** (Trees in prohibited zones without Austin Energy approval): **NOT-VERIFIABLE** - Cannot verify without species identification and distance confirmation\n\nLet me output the findings for the items that are FAIL or NOT-VERIFIABLE:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3930,"cache_read_input_tokens":69178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3930},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2af985ee-646f-4363-9e8d-5974ccc4a371","uuid":"b09a6d0f-8c41-44a6-8102-185decdabd7f"}
{"level":30,"time":1776970186342,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01WWAeBeSRuLNVonFUtL1DLz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is very helpful information! Let me summarize what I've learned so far:\n\n## Key Findings About the Electric Infrastructure:\n\n### From Sheet 3 (General Notes):\n- **Overhead Electric Transmission Easement** exists on the property\n- **25-foot no-dig/grade zone** from transmission structures\n- **25-foot no-tree zone** from transmission structures\n- Pre-construction coordination required with Austin Energy\n- Roads within easement must support 46,000 lb tandem axle\n- Fire hydrants must be 20 feet from transmission structures\n- Sprinklers prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles\n- No habitable structures, permanent structures, or drainage/detention ponds permitted in easement\n\n### From Sheet 4 (Existing Conditions Survey):\n- **5' and 10' Electric Easements** marked on northern property boundary (Vol. 4443, Pg. 76 P.R.T.C.TX)\n- **5' Electric Easements** on southern property boundary\n- **Overhead Utility Lines (O.H.U.L.)** routing visible:\n  - Along Valley View Road (southern/western boundary)\n  - Service drop crossing from pole on Valley View Road over existing building\n  - Along northern boundary within marked easements\n- **Power poles** at multiple locations (northwest, north-central, northeast, southwest corners and along road)\n- **Guy wires** at several pole locations\n\n### From Sheet 9 (Site Plan - Proposed):\n- **Overhead Electric Lines (OHE, OH)** marked on eastern and southern perimeters\n- **5-foot Electric Easements** along eastern and southern boundaries\n- **Buildings 6, 7, 8** are closest to electric easements but appear set back outside easement boundaries\n- **Retaining walls** appear to be positioned at or just outside easement boundaries\n- **Biofiltration/Detention Pond** in northeast corner, but note about 15' and 25' drainage easements (not electrical)\n\n\n- Driveways and parking areas positioned away from perimeter utilities.\n\nThere's a potential conflict: Sheet 3 explicitly prohibits detention ponds within the transmission easement, yet the proposed site plan shows a detention pond in the northeast corner. I need to determine if this pond falls within the transmission easement boundaries mentioned in Sheet 3.\n\nThe distinction matters—Sheet 9 references only 5-foot distribution easements, which are different from the transmission easement with its 25-foot no-dig restriction noted on Sheet 3. Checking the Electrical Design Plan (Sheet 21) and Landscape Plan (Sheet 31) should clarify the underground infrastructure layout and whether the detention pond placement creates an actual violation or if it's positioned outside the restricted zone.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3181,"cache_read_input_tokens":53419,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3181},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"70b192e9-3480-432d-b26e-17170fe74ff5","uuid":"c0d90bc3-3c67-4186-9bf3-79c15c159180"}
