{"level":30,"time":1776970755024,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01PJLS6Yi7dmDyvDMzKJLdj4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Based on my research, I've found:\n\n1. The project has 5-foot electric easements on the property (from facts.md)\n2. The landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows an overhead electric (OHE) line on the southern property boundary, running parallel to and just outside the property\n3. The grading plan (Sheet 11) does NOT explicitly show the overhead electric line, but does show 5' electric easements\n4. The existing conditions survey (Sheet 4) does NOT show any overhead electric facilities - only underground utilities and easements\n5. There are no building elevation sheets showing the overhead line and vertical clearances\n\nThis is critical information. The grouping file (EL-MD-exp/1.md) requires multiple assessments of overhead electric facilities. Let me now evaluate each checklist item:\n\n**Applicability Assessment:**\nThe site plan shows:\n- Proposed buildings and structures\n- An overhead electric (OHE) line on/adjacent to the southern property boundary\n- No overhead poles explicitly shown on the site plan\n- No clear indication of the existence/characteristics of the overhead line beyond the landscape plan label\n\nThe landscape plan showed \"OHE\" on the southern boundary, but:\n- No dimensions showing clearances from the overhead line to buildings\n- No elevation data for the overhead line\n- No vertical clearance verification exhibits\n- No profile plans showing the overhead line in elevation\n- No survey data showing conductor locations or mid-span clearances\n\nNow I need to evaluate each checklist item. Since the grouping is about \"Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines,\" and there ARE overhead electric facilities shown (at least the OHE line on the landscape plan), the grouping is APPLICABLE.\n\nHowever, the applicability of INDIVIDUAL checklist items depends on:\n1. Whether overhead facilities exist on or adjacent to the site (YES - the OHE line)\n2. What specific site features are proposed (buildings, fences, driveways, landscaping, etc.)\n3. Whether clearance requirements apply to those specific features\n\n\n4. Whether the plans provide sufficient documentation to verify compliance\n\nFor EL-1.1 regarding building clearances from overhead conductors: The proposed structures and the adjacent OHE line mean this item applies. However, the plans lack the necessary elevation drawings and clearance dimensions to verify the 7.5-foot horizontal separation requirement. Without surveyed conductor locations or building elevations annotated with clearance data, I cannot confirm compliance—this appears to be a documentation gap rather than a design failure. -to-ground clearance. Since buildings are proposed and overhead facilities exist, this applies. The elevation sheets lack surveyed conductor heights and any clearance dimensions, which constitutes missing required documentation—a fail.\n\n**EL-1.2** requires 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductors for customer facilities. The plans don't specify whether the overhead line is distribution or transmission, and no radial clearance dimensions appear anywhere. This missing verification is a fail.\n\n**EL-1.3** addresses building projections and their required clearances from the overhead line. The elevation drawings provide no information about projections or their distances from the overhead conductors, which is another documentation gap.\n\nGas pump canopies don't appear on the site plan, so **EL-1.4** doesn't apply. For **EL-1.5**, the site includes concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, and pedestrian walkways that could function as elevated platforms, yet no profile plans show clearances to the overhead line. **EL-1.6** applies because buildings are proposed near an overhead line that appears to run along or adjacent to an easement on the southern boundary, but permanent clearance documentation is absent. **EL-1.7** requires surveyed elevations and conductor locations on building elevation sheets—the plan set doesn't include these sheets at all.\n\nSection and plan views need to show dimensions from the outer conductor, which they don't. Wooden privacy fences are shown on the landscape plan but lack clearance dimensions relative to the overhead line. Pole locations aren't clearly marked on the grading plan, making it uncertain whether the 10-foot fence post clearance requirement applies. No substation is indicated, so fencing near substations isn't relevant. Transmission line fencing requirements need further evaluation based on the specific overhead facilities present.\n\nVehicle barriers beneath the overhead line may be needed given the driveways and parking areas shown, but no clearance documentation exists if they're proposed. Retaining walls are explicitly shown on the landscape plan, yet there are no section views demonstrating adequate clearance from the overhead line to these structures.\n\nNo substation or emergency access structures appear on the plans, so those items don't apply. However, the proposed driveways require verification of vertical clearance where the overhead line may cross them—the landscape plan indicates the OHE runs along the southern boundary while driveways are positioned internally and at the southern edge, but no section views provide the necessary clearance dimensions.\n\nParking areas exist beneath or near the OHE line without documented clearance measurements. The transmission line voltage is unknown, making it unclear whether road grade clearance standards apply. Valley View Road qualifies as a local street rather than a highway, so those requirements don't trigger. No poles are shown on the site plan. Most critically, no clearance dimensions are measured from the conductor anywhere in the documentation, which represents a fundamental gap in the required technical exhibits.\n\nFor streetlight poles, the photometric plan suggests they may be present and could potentially fall beneath the OHE line—this needs verification. Trash enclosures are documented on Sheet 9 but lack any clearance verification. The site plan indicates structures in the southern area where the OHE line runs, and I need to confirm whether any facilities are positioned directly beneath or over the distribution line, as this would be prohibited.\n\nWater infrastructure on site requires clearance documentation that isn't provided—both the meters and water lines adjacent to the OHE line show no dimensional analysis. Buildings positioned near the property line where overhead facilities occupy the easement lack any foundation setback analysis. Additionally, the FFE elevations (683-687.5) don't correlate with any shown OHE line elevation, and there's no registered surveyor documentation of mid-span clearances.\n\nFor the remaining standards: overhead line crossing angles can't be verified from the plans. Only a single OHE line appears, so secondary-to-primary conflicts don't apply. No marquee signs or power poles are indicated on the drawings. Decorative lighting beneath the overhead lines may be proposed but lacks clearance documentation, resulting in a failure. Tree plantings shown on the landscape plan present a significant issue—the drawings don't indicate lateral distances from trees to conductors or confirm species compatibility with utility requirements, which is required. Electrical meters are depicted on the design plan and need verification that they're not obstructed.\n\nFor streetlights adjacent to the property, I can't determine applicability without additional site information. Utility poles aren't indicated on the existing conditions survey despite the legend showing the symbol, so guy wire and guy anchor requirements don't apply. Transformers need to maintain proper vertical clearance from structures and vegetation.\n\nTransformer pads appear on the electrical plan but lack elevation details showing clearance from overhead lines—this is a failure. Whether transformers sit in niche configurations remains unclear from available documentation. Construction staging areas should maintain adequate separation, though no dedicated clearance plan was provided, making this difficult to verify. Since overhead lines exist and construction is planned, the 48-hour notification requirement applies and references Texas Health code provisions.\n\nThe service configuration uses underground delivery rather than overhead masts, so roof-penetration clearances don't apply. I need to verify the cover sheet and general notes contain the required notification language, check all sheets for clearance documentation, and confirm the scope of overhead electrical infrastructure on the property.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46195,"cache_read_input_tokens":19314,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":46195},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a582675-f45b-4e40-ac49-d1379f357a0d","uuid":"d904bcc3-fff2-4239-9966-79409257ad31"}
