{"level":30,"time":1776970758901,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CWgqDquPqe3yKJU4tk5yvp","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 35 — Landscape Calculations\n     2→\n     3→Provides landscape calculations, plant lists, and tree preservation notes.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains landscape and tree preservation calculations, plant schedules, and standard municipal notes for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to demonstrate compliance with City of Austin landscape, buffering, and tree mitigation requirements, detailing exact plant species, quantities, and associated fees for tree removal.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Site & Landscape Areas:** Total Site Area: 94,203 SF; Total Street-Yard Area: 6,314 SF; Street-Yard Landscape Provided: 5,206 SF (Required: 1,263 SF)\n    11→*   **Open Space & Buffering:** Private Common Open Space Provided: 5,855 SF (Required: 4,715 SF); Buffer Points Provided: 57 (Required: 40)\n    12→*   **Parking Lot Landscape:** Non-Street-Yard Landscape Provided: 816 SF (Required: 60 SF)\n    13→*   **Tree Preservation & Mitigation:** Total App F Tree Inches Surveyed: 606 inches; Total App F Tree Inches Removed: 240 inches; Total Invasive Tree Inches Removed: 137 inches\n    14→*   **Mitigation Requirements:** Total Mitigation Inches Planted on Site: 55 inches; Remaining Mitigation Inches Required: 162 inches; Amount to be Paid into Fund: $26,900\n    15→*   **Key Plant Quantities (Minimum specs):** 10 Cedar Elm (100 gal.), 2 Live Oak (100 gal.), 6 Mexican Redbud (45 gal.), 2 Texas Mountain Laurel (45 gal.)\n    16→*   **Groundcover Quantities:** 92 Obedient Plant (4\" flat), 72 Frogfruit (4\" flat)\n    17→\n    18→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    19→\n    20→**Calculations & Mitigation Data**\n    21→*   **Block 14:** City of Austin Landscape Calculations. READ for required versus provided area metrics for street yards, parking lots, buffering points, and private common open space.\n    22→*   **Blocks 11 & 17:** Tree Preservation Calculations (Heading and Table). READ for an exact breakdown of existing trees, trees removed by size/category, replacement inches required (including 300% multipliers for heritage trees), and final fee-in-lieu calculations. \n    23→*   **Block 15:** COA Required Trees Chart. READ for street yard, buffer, and parking lot tree inch requirements (Total required: 13 inches) versus proposed inches. \n    24→*   **Block 3:** Tree preservation notes. READ for exemptions (Chinaberry, Bradford Pear, and Ash trees require no mitigation).\n    25→\n    26→**Plant List Schedules**\n    27→*   **Blocks 6 & 16:** Plant List heading and Shade Trees schedule. READ for exact shade tree species, quantities (10 Cedar Elm, 2 Live Oak), and minimum planting specifications (100 Gal., 4\" Cal.). Use VISION to see the associated plan symbols.\n    28→*   **Block 12:** Ornamental Trees schedule. READ for species, quantities, and container specs. Use VISION to map symbols to the landscape plan.\n    29→*   **Block 18:** Shrubs schedule. READ for shrub species, quantities, container sizes, and maximum spacing requirements. \n    30→*   **Block 13:** Groundcovers schedule. READ for groundcover species, quantities, and spacing specifications.\n    31→*   **Block 5:** Plant list notes. READ for contractor instructions regarding unit cost formatting, substitutions, and plant sourcing responsibilities.\n    32→\n    33→**Municipal Notes & Specifications**\n    34→*   **Block 1:** Remedial Tree Care Notes. READ for aeration and supplemental nutrient requirements for trees within construction areas.\n    35→*   **Block 2:** Special Construction Techniques (ECM 3.5.4 D). READ for specifications on rock sawing, mulch applications, and grading near critical root zones.\n    36→*   **Block 4:** Modified & Natural Area Protection Notes. READ for fencing, wrapping, and pruning rules before, during, and after construction.\n    37→*   **Block 7:** Site Development Permit Landscape notes. READ for requirements on protective barriers, vegetation buffering, and temporary seeding during specific dates.\n    38→*   **Block 8:** Site Development Permit Irrigation notes. READ for TCEQ Chapter 344 compliance, water conservation rules, and final inspection deliverable requirements.\n    39→\n    40→**Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    41→*   **Blocks 9, 10, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24:** Project and sheet administrative data. READ for project name (Valley View Townhomes), owner (WM 41 Austin RE, LLC), location (3759 Valley View Road), engineering firm (Blu Fish Collaborative), landscape architect seal, and sheet number (32 of 35).\n    42→\n    43→---\n    44→\n    45→- `block-1.md` — notes: Notes on remedial tree care, aeration, and supplemental nutrient requirements.\n    46→- `block-2.md` — notes: Notes related to site development permit irrigation.\n    47→- `block-3.md` — notes: Notes on modified and natural area protection.\n    48→- `block-7.md` — notes: Notes related to site development permit landscape.\n    49→\n    50→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    51→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f67b20d3-b17d-4418-aa28-3e2e17c1cd9f","uuid":"2674007d-5382-4a13-b19c-786a4c2b8ae3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-35/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 35 — Landscape Calculations\n\nProvides landscape calculations, plant lists, and tree preservation notes.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains landscape and tree preservation calculations, plant schedules, and standard municipal notes for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to demonstrate compliance with City of Austin landscape, buffering, and tree mitigation requirements, detailing exact plant species, quantities, and associated fees for tree removal.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Site & Landscape Areas:** Total Site Area: 94,203 SF; Total Street-Yard Area: 6,314 SF; Street-Yard Landscape Provided: 5,206 SF (Required: 1,263 SF)\n*   **Open Space & Buffering:** Private Common Open Space Provided: 5,855 SF (Required: 4,715 SF); Buffer Points Provided: 57 (Required: 40)\n*   **Parking Lot Landscape:** Non-Street-Yard Landscape Provided: 816 SF (Required: 60 SF)\n*   **Tree Preservation & Mitigation:** Total App F Tree Inches Surveyed: 606 inches; Total App F Tree Inches Removed: 240 inches; Total Invasive Tree Inches Removed: 137 inches\n*   **Mitigation Requirements:** Total Mitigation Inches Planted on Site: 55 inches; Remaining Mitigation Inches Required: 162 inches; Amount to be Paid into Fund: $26,900\n*   **Key Plant Quantities (Minimum specs):** 10 Cedar Elm (100 gal.), 2 Live Oak (100 gal.), 6 Mexican Redbud (45 gal.), 2 Texas Mountain Laurel (45 gal.)\n*   **Groundcover Quantities:** 92 Obedient Plant (4\" flat), 72 Frogfruit (4\" flat)\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n**Calculations & Mitigation Data**\n*   **Block 14:** City of Austin Landscape Calculations. READ for required versus provided area metrics for street yards, parking lots, buffering points, and private common open space.\n*   **Blocks 11 & 17:** Tree Preservation Calculations (Heading and Table). READ for an exact breakdown of existing trees, trees removed by size/category, replacement inches required (including 300% multipliers for heritage trees), and final fee-in-lieu calculations. \n*   **Block 15:** COA Required Trees Chart. READ for street yard, buffer, and parking lot tree inch requirements (Total required: 13 inches) versus proposed inches. \n*   **Block 3:** Tree preservation notes. READ for exemptions (Chinaberry, Bradford Pear, and Ash trees require no mitigation).\n\n**Plant List Schedules**\n*   **Blocks 6 & 16:** Plant List heading and Shade Trees schedule. READ for exact shade tree species, quantities (10 Cedar Elm, 2 Live Oak), and minimum planting specifications (100 Gal., 4\" Cal.). Use VISION to see the associated plan symbols.\n*   **Block 12:** Ornamental Trees schedule. READ for species, quantities, and container specs. Use VISION to map symbols to the landscape plan.\n*   **Block 18:** Shrubs schedule. READ for shrub species, quantities, container sizes, and maximum spacing requirements. \n*   **Block 13:** Groundcovers schedule. READ for groundcover species, quantities, and spacing specifications.\n*   **Block 5:** Plant list notes. READ for contractor instructions regarding unit cost formatting, substitutions, and plant sourcing responsibilities.\n\n**Municipal Notes & Specifications**\n*   **Block 1:** Remedial Tree Care Notes. READ for aeration and supplemental nutrient requirements for trees within construction areas.\n*   **Block 2:** Special Construction Techniques (ECM 3.5.4 D). READ for specifications on rock sawing, mulch applications, and grading near critical root zones.\n*   **Block 4:** Modified & Natural Area Protection Notes. READ for fencing, wrapping, and pruning rules before, during, and after construction.\n*   **Block 7:** Site Development Permit Landscape notes. READ for requirements on protective barriers, vegetation buffering, and temporary seeding during specific dates.\n*   **Block 8:** Site Development Permit Irrigation notes. READ for TCEQ Chapter 344 compliance, water conservation rules, and final inspection deliverable requirements.\n\n**Boilerplate & Administrative**\n*   **Blocks 9, 10, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24:** Project and sheet administrative data. READ for project name (Valley View Townhomes), owner (WM 41 Austin RE, LLC), location (3759 Valley View Road), engineering firm (Blu Fish Collaborative), landscape architect seal, and sheet number (32 of 35).\n\n---\n\n- `block-1.md` — notes: Notes on remedial tree care, aeration, and supplemental nutrient requirements.\n- `block-2.md` — notes: Notes related to site development permit irrigation.\n- `block-3.md` — notes: Notes on modified and natural area protection.\n- `block-7.md` — notes: Notes related to site development permit landscape.\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776970758919,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HJTgdMkTxZBYduVKPNXDUV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n     4→\n     5→Americans with Disabilities Act\n     6→The City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n     7→\n     8→## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n     9→\n    10→Legal Description\n    11→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    12→\n    13→## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n    14→\n    15→Site Plan Release Notes\n    16→1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n    17→2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n    18→3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n    19→4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n    20→5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    21→6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n    22→8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    23→9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    24→10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    25→\n    26→## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n    27→\n    28→Fire Department\n    29→1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n    30→2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n    31→3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n    32→4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n    33→5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n    34→6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n    35→7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n    36→\n    37→## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n    38→\n    39→Benchmarks\n    40→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    41→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    42→\n    43→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    44→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a582675-f45b-4e40-ac49-d1379f357a0d","uuid":"ce6b3e2f-42f3-4858-8a7f-21bcc8a08d4d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n\nAmericans with Disabilities Act\nThe City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n\n## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n\nSite Plan Release Notes\n1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n\n## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n\nFire Department\n1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n\n## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776970759274,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011FvZQLoUkXSYMASB9X7Mco","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! The measurement results are very revealing:\n\nPair 0 (Westernmost tree): 0.8 feet - CRITICAL VIOLATION\nPair 1 (Second tree): 107 feet - Well clear\nPair 2 (Third tree): 0 feet - ON the OHE line - CRITICAL VIOLATION  \nPair 3 (Easternmost tree): 0 feet - ON the OHE line - CRITICAL VIOLATION\n\nSo three of the four M trees have serious clearance violations:\n- Tree 1: 0.8 feet from OHE (requires 15 feet minimum for non-UC trees, or 25 feet for large trees)\n- Tree 3: 0 feet from OHE (directly on the line)\n- Tree 4: 0 feet from OHE (directly on the line)\n\nThese clearances are far below the minimum requirements of:\n- 15 lateral feet for non-utility-compatible trees from distribution conductors\n- 25 lateral feet for large trees (40+ ft mature height) from distribution conductors\n\nThis is a major compliance issue. Trees cannot be planted directly on utility conductors.\n\nHowever, I still have a problem: I don't know what species these trees are. Without species identification, I technically cannot determine if they violate the specific requirements for non-utility-compatible trees, but the violation is clear regardless because:\n1. NO trees of any kind are permitted on the OHE line itself\n2. At 0.8 feet clearance, even utility-compatible trees would violate the 10-foot minimum clearance from poles (if there's a pole there)\n\nEven if these were utility-compatible trees, they're still being planted within prohibited zones.\n\nLet me now check what I know:\n- The landscape plan shows four M (mitigation) trees near/on the OHE\n- Three of them are positioned directly on or almost on the OHE line\n- No species identification provided on the landscape plan\n- No clearance dimensions shown\n\n\n- The plant schedule on Sheet 35 doesn't cross-reference species to specific locations\n\nThis creates a dual problem: I can identify the clearance violations with certainty, but cannot pinpoint whether they breach EL-2.1 (non-UC trees) or EL-2.3 (large trees) requirements without species data. Regardless, planting trees directly on overhead electric lines is fundamentally non-compliant, and the landscape plan also fails to specify which species are being used.\n\nThe OHE appears on the plan but lacks the documentation needed to verify utility compatibility—no dimension lines, no species labels, and the measurements suggest the placement may be geometrically impossible. This prevents adequate clearance verification as required. Additionally, there are no dimensions showing whether measurements originate from the conductor or from the trunk, making it impossible to confirm compliance with either EL-2.12 or EL-2.13. Looking more closely at the measurements, three of the trees appear to sit directly on or extremely close to the OHE line itself, which indicates either a significant design error, misplacement of the trees on the plan, or that the plan is too schematic to be reliable. Without species identification and with trees positioned essentially on the conductor line, I need to flag both EL-2.10 and EL-2.14 as failures—the plant schedule lacks species designation for the four mitigation trees at the southern boundary, and their positioning directly on the OHE line with no clearance dimensions shown violates the requirement to adequately demonstrate clearance distances.\n\nThe measured positioning of 0-0.8 feet from the OHE is itself a violation regardless of species, since any tree type requires a minimum 15-foot clearance from distribution lines. While the checklist structure focuses on specific tree categories (utility-compatible versus non-compatible, large versus small), the fundamental issue here is that the trees are positioned far too close to the overhead line to meet any clearance standard.\n\nThe primary failures are: EL-2.10 for lack of species identification, EL-2.14 for missing clearance dimensions, and EL-2.1 for the clearance violation itself—which exists independent of whether these are utility-compatible trees, since the measured distances fall well below the required 15-foot minimum.\n\nI should systematically work through the checklist items. EL-2.1 applies since distribution lines are present and trees are positioned within 15 feet, but I cannot verify the non-utility-compatible status without species data. The measured clearances of 0-0.8 feet versus the 15-25 foot requirement represent a clear violation regardless.\n\nFor EL-2.2, the OHE appears to be distribution rather than transmission based on the easement widths noted, so this likely doesn't apply. EL-2.3 is similarly not verifiable without knowing mature heights, though the proximity measurements show trees well within the 25-foot threshold. EL-2.4 doesn't apply since transmission lines aren't present.\n\nEL-2.5 passes—no transmission easement is documented on the plans. However, EL-2.6 presents a clear issue: trees are positioned 0-0.8 feet from utility poles, well within the prohibited 10-foot buffer. For EL-2.7, I need to determine whether pad-mounted transformers visible on the electrical plan are also shown on the landscape plan to assess compliance with the 10-foot setback requirement.\n\nThe underground equipment clearances (EL-2.8 and EL-2.9) cannot be verified because pull boxes and vaults aren't adequately identified on the landscape plan. EL-2.10 fails because four medium trees near the overhead line lack species identification, and the plant schedule doesn't correlate to specific plan locations. EL-2.11 requires checking whether large trees are properly distinguished from smaller specimens.\n\nClearance measurements (EL-2.12 and EL-2.13) are not applicable since no dimensions appear on the plan. EL-2.14 fails—while trees and overhead equipment are both shown, the lack of species labels and clearance dimensions means the tree-to-OHE relationship cannot be adequately verified, particularly given the minimal 0-0.8 ft spacing observed. EL-2.15 addresses whether trees occupy prohibited zones without proper authorization. Trees are positioned within restricted clearance areas, but no Austin Energy written approval is documented, resulting in a failure.\n\nThe primary failures stem from missing species identification (EL-2.10), trees within 10 feet of poles (EL-2.6), inadequate documentation of tree positioning relative to overhead facilities (EL-2.14), and lack of utility approval for trees in restricted zones (EL-2.15). Several items cannot be verified without species data or equipment locations—non-utility trees within 15 feet, large trees within 25 feet, and trees near underground equipment all depend on information not provided on the landscape plan.\n\nRegarding EL-2.7, I should reconsider whether transformer pads shown on the electrical plan align with tree locations on the landscape plan. The electrical plan identifies three transformer pads, but the landscape plan doesn't clearly indicate their positions relative to proposed trees, making it difficult to assess the 10-foot clearance requirement.\n\nFor EL-2.8 and EL-2.9, underground equipment like pull boxes and transformers appear on the electrical plan, but their exact locations aren't clearly marked on the landscape plan in relation to tree placements. Without this spatial relationship, I can't verify compliance.\n\nLooking back at EL-2.1 through EL-2.4, the measured distances from overhead equipment (0-0.8 feet) fall significantly short of required minimums—15 feet for non-UC trees and 25 feet for large trees. Regardless of whether I can pinpoint specific species, the clearance violations are evident from the measurements alone.\n\nEL-2.15 provides a more comprehensive framework: it flags any trees positioned within prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy approval. These zones span multiple distances depending on equipment type—15 feet for distribution lines with non-UC trees, 25 feet for large trees, 50 feet for transmission lines, and various distances for poles and underground equipment. The 0-0.8 foot clearance clearly violates these thresholds, placing trees within the prohibited zones regardless of whether they're classified as non-UC or large.\n\nMy assessment identifies four definitive failures: EL-2.6 for proximity to utility poles, EL-2.10 for missing species data, EL-2.14 for inadequate clearance documentation, and EL-2.15 for trees in restricted zones without approval. Five items remain not-verifiable due to incomplete species identification or unmarked equipment locations on the landscape plan—EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.8, EL-2.9, and EL-2.11. The remaining standards don't apply to this scenario. I should also reconsider whether EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 warrant further evaluation based on their specific requirements. L-2.13 are N/A since no clearances are dimensioned on the plans. The applicability criteria explicitly states these items only apply when tree clearances are shown with dimensions—they're evaluating the measurement method, not whether clearances should exist at all.\n\nFor EL-2.14, I need to verify my understanding. The item flags when trees aren't positioned relative to overhead electric facilities on the landscape plan, which prevents confirming adequate clearance. The applicability requires overhead electric facilities to be present or proposed, and the check involves comparing the landscape plan against utility and site plans to confirm all overhead conductors are accounted for.\n\nLooking at what's shown: the landscape plan does include OHE facilities and trees, but lacks species identification, dimension lines, or any clear spatial relationship between them. The field measurements indicate trees are essentially aligned with the OHE line—either indicating a significant clearance problem or that the plan is too schematic to be reliable. Either interpretation means the trees aren't depicted with the clarity needed to verify compliance. This fails the requirement.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5933,"cache_read_input_tokens":51260,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5933},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21547d2b-78a2-4a78-a9a7-593ead7a7df9","uuid":"413f1a7a-c4be-49ac-85cc-2f8da5704122"}
{"level":30,"time":1776970760203,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Examine the Landscape Plan for trees and their proximity to transformer pad locations shown on the electrical plan (Sheet 21). Provide:\n\n1. How many trees are shown on the landscape plan?\n2. What are the tree species and sizes indicated?\n3. Are tree locations marked with plant symbols and labels (species codes like \"SY\", \"PL\", \"P\", \"M\")?\n4. Where are trees positioned relative to the main driveway/internal circulation loop on the west/central portion of the site?\n5. Are there any trees or shrubs shown within 10 lateral feet of the transformer pad locations (which on Sheet 21 are located: Pad 1 NW, Pad 2 W-Central, Pad 3 SW, Pad 4 E-Central, Pad 5 SE)?\n6. Are there landscape features (groundcover, shrubs) shown near transformer locations?\n7. Are root barriers indicated for any trees?\n8. Are there planting details or notes about tree species compatibility with utilities?\n9. What is the distance from planted trees to the nearest paved surfaces/driveways?\n10. Are overhead utility lines or electric easement areas noted on the landscape plan?\n\nUse compass directions and visual estimates based on the scale 1\" = 20'.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a careful visual analysis of the provided Landscape Plan (Sheet 28 of 35), here are the findings corresponding to your questions. \n\n**Note:** The electrical transformer pads mentioned in your prompt (from Sheet 21) are not explicitly labeled or drawn on this specific landscape plan. Analysis regarding proximity to these pads is based solely on the general directional regions (NW, W-Central, SW, E-Central, SE) of the site as shown on this sheet.\n\n**1. How many trees are shown on the landscape plan?**\nThere are dozens of trees depicted on the plan, categor