{"level":30,"time":1776970788214,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_016k8Xti5mPC1Uj99U5bbSJk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Block 17 appears to be a duplicate of Block 19 with similar content. I've now reviewed all the electric notes on Sheet 3 and I can see:\n\n1. There are Electric Notes (Block 19) that address clearances and responsibility for maintaining clearances\n2. There are Electric Transmission Notes (Block 13) for transmission lines that require a 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting\n3. There is an Austin Energy note in Block 17 that also addresses clearances\n\nHowever, I do NOT see a general 48-hour notification requirement (per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003) that applies to ALL overhead lines (not just transmission). This is specifically required by EL-1.46.\n\nNow let me compile my comprehensive research summary:\n\n## Research Summary for EL-MD-Exp Grouping 1: Site Feature Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines\n\n### Overhead Electric Infrastructure Found:\n1. **One Overhead Electric (OHE) Line** shown on Landscape Plan (Sheet 31), running along/adjacent to the southern property boundary\n2. **Electric Easements:** 5-foot electric easements noted on property (facts.md shows \"Easement 7 & 8 — 5' Electric Easements (×2)\" in Mecey Subdivision plat)\n3. **Underground Electric Infrastructure:** Proposed underground electric lines, transformer pads, pull boxes, and meters shown on Electrical Design Plan (Sheet 21)\n4. **No Overhead Poles** explicitly shown on any site plan sheets\n5. **No Surveyed Conductor Elevations** or vertical clearance data provided\n\n### Critical Findings - What's Missing:\n1. **NO Building Elevation Sheets** - No architectural elevations showing building faces relative to overhead conductors\n2. **NO Vertical Clearance Dimensions** - No section views with dimension annotations from overhead line to buildings, driveways, parking, or other site features\n\n\n3. The OHE line lacks voltage classification and type identification, making it impossible to determine if it's distribution or transmission level. Additionally, there's no surveyed conductor data showing actual locations and elevations, no clearance verification for the extensive tree plantings shown on the landscape plan, and no section or profile views to verify clearances. The plans are also missing the required 48-hour notification note for contractor work near high-voltage lines per Texas Health & Safety Code, and critical dimension annotations are absent throughout.\n\nThe site has multiple features requiring clearance verification—Buildings 6, 7, and 8 along with parking structures on the southern portion, retaining walls, proposed driveways and parking areas, landscape plantings, and underground utilities. According to the applicable standards, I need to confirm 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance, 15-foot radial clearance from distribution conductors, 16-18 foot vertical clearance for driveways, and 10 lateral feet for trees, but none of these clearances are dimensioned or verified on the current plans.\n\nThe electric notes acknowledge developer responsibility for maintaining clearances and Austin Energy's right to prune trees, along with the developer's obligation to prune vegetation within 10 feet of the overhead line centerline. However, these notes lack the specific code-required clearance dimensions and don't constitute engineering verification. Since overhead electric infrastructure exists adjacent to the site with multiple proposed features positioned near or on the southern area where the lines are located, this grouping applies and requires clearance verification on the plans.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":