{"level":30,"time":1776971405293,"pid":55640,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01JdqFakkRhMFqMchJLiEmp2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is crucial - the cover sheet does NOT have the required 48-hour notification note. The grouping states that per EL-1.46, there must be a note that \"owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines\" per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003.\n\nWhile Sheet 3 (General Notes) does mention this requirement in the \"Electric Transmission Notes\", it needs to be on the cover sheet or clearly visible general notes. Let me verify if Sheet 3 is technically \"general notes\" that would be part of the overall plan requirements.\n\nActually, looking back at the README, Sheet 3 is titled \"General Notes\" so it should count. However, EL-1.46 specifically says \"Plans do not include required note\" - and the note IS on Sheet 3. So this should be PASS unless it's only on sheet 3 and not on the cover sheet.\n\nActually, re-reading EL-1.46 from the grouping: \"Plans do not include required note that owner/contractor must notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before work begins near high-voltage overhead lines\"\n\nThe note IS included on Sheet 3. So this would be PASS, unless the requirement is that it must be on the cover sheet. The grouping doesn't specify \"on cover sheet\" - it just says on \"site plans\".\n\nNow let me compile my comprehensive findings. I've gathered extensive evidence. Let me systematically go through each checklist item:\n\n## COMPREHENSIVE EVALUATION OF ALL 47 ITEMS:\n\n**EL-1.1** - Buildings and permanent structures do not maintain minimum 7 feet 6 inches horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric conductors\n- **Applicability:** YES - buildings proposed near OHE on southern boundary\n- **Finding:** No surveyed conductor elevations shown; no building elevation sheets with conductor elevations\n- **Status:** NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n\n- **EL-1.2** - Customer facilities lack the required 15-foot radial clearance from distribution primary and neutral conductors. The plans don't document conductor type or provide radial clearance measurements. **EL-1.3** - Building projections like roofs and overhangs aren't shown with elevation details that would verify clearances from overhead lines.\n\n**EL-1.4** - Gas pump canopies are not part of this proposal, so this requirement doesn't apply.\n\n**EL-1.5** - No elevated walkways or platforms are proposed beyond accessible ramps, making this non-applicable.\n\n**EL-1.6** - Buildings must maintain 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric facilities in the right-of-way adjacent to the site.\n\nBuildings are positioned near the ROW with overhead electric lines present, but no clearance verification or surveyed data has been provided to confirm compliance. The building elevation sheets required to demonstrate these clearances are missing from the site plan, and no surveyed conductor elevations are included. Additionally, section and plan view exhibits showing dimensions from the outer electric conductor to the outermost edge of buildings are absent, with the site plan containing no clearance dimension callouts from conductors to structures.\n\nFor fencing, perimeter fences are visible on Sheets 4 and 9, but no clearance dimensions are shown relative to overhead conductors or poles. Fence posts lack documented clearance verification from overhead electric poles in the ROW at property corners. No substation fences are shown on or adjacent to the site, so electrical isolation requirements do not apply.\n\nRegarding transmission lines, Sheet 3 contains notes about them, but verification shows only distribution lines on the southern and western boundaries—the transmission references appear to be standard boilerplate language not applicable to this specific site. No vehicle barrier fencing beneath overhead lines is proposed, so those clearance requirements are not triggered.\n\nFor retaining walls, the site plan shows them around the detention pond and perimeter areas. I need to verify their proximity to the overhead electric lines documented along the southern boundary on the landscape plan. The Austin Energy substation requirement doesn't apply since no substation appears on the site plan. Emergency access structures also need evaluation for the required clearance standards.\n\nRetaining wall sections would be necessary if they're positioned near overhead lines, showing dimensions from the outer conductor to the wall's edge. For the driveway clearance requirement, the grading plan and profiles don't indicate vertical clearance measurements, which would be needed for both standard parking areas and truck traffic zones.\n\nParking pads also lack documented clearance verification from the lowest overhead lines. Regarding transmission lines, the site notes reference them but only distribution lines are confirmed present, so that standard doesn't apply here. For highways or freeways with underbuilt distribution, a 40-foot vertical clearance would be required if applicable.\n\nThe site is a local development rather than a highway or freeway project, so that standard doesn't apply. Existing poles sit in the right-of-way at property corners while driveways are internal to the site, meaning no actual conflicts exist between poles and proposed driveway paths. When clearance dimensions are shown, they need to be measured from the outside conductor rather than from the pole itself—but since no clearance dimensions appear on these plans, I can't verify the measurement reference point.\n\nStreetlight poles are positioned on the internal site away from overhead electric lines. New sidewalks are proposed along Valley View Road's right-of-way where poles already exist at the property corners, so I need to verify the sidewalk layout doesn't conflict with these pole locations. The trash enclosure shown on the site plan appears positioned away from overhead electric facilities. Customer facilities proposed for installation need to be checked against overhead electric line locations.\n\nElectric easements exist on the property boundaries with a 5-foot easement marked on the eastern boundary. I need to confirm that proposed improvements don't encroach within the easement or violate the minimum 7.5-foot clearance requirement from conductors. Water meters are shown on the site plan but lack clearance dimensions to the overhead service drops, making it impossible to verify compliance with the 7.5-foot horizontal clearance standard. Water lines are underground per Sheet 21, so they don't conflict with overhead electrical facilities. Buildings are positioned near the southern and eastern property lines, but foundation details don't show whether setbacks provide adequate space for electric facility requirements. A registered surveyor's survey documenting pole locations and mid-span clearances hasn't been provided, which is needed to verify vertical clearance compliance for driveways and parking areas.\n\nNo new overhead electric lines are proposed to cross roadways, and existing lines in the right-of-way aren't being repositioned, so that standard doesn't apply. Similarly, without a detailed utility plan showing overhead line routing, I can't determine if secondary lines would cross primary lines.\n\nFor the remaining standards: no marquee signs are proposed, eliminating that concern. Underground service is planned rather than new power poles, so spacing requirements between poles don't apply. However, decorative lighting under existing overhead lines does warrant attention—the site plan indicates all exterior lighting will be full cut-off and shielded per code requirements.\n\nRegarding trees near overhead electric facilities, the landscape plan shows multiple species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel) positioned along the southern boundary where overhead lines exist. Documentation confirming these species meet utility compatibility standards from the applicable appendix is not provided, making verification impossible at this stage.\n\nFor meter accessibility, transformers and meters appear in landscaped areas on the electrical plans, but no clearance verification for maintenance access has been demonstrated.\n\nRegarding streetlights, no existing fixtures were identified adjacent to the site, so this criterion doesn't apply. For guy wire conflicts, the survey indicates guy wires near poles in the right-of-way, though verification that these don't interfere with proposed accessible routes and driveways is missing—utility infrastructure details are often incomplete in submissions. Guy easements extending the required distances from anchor points also lack documentation.\n\nThe existing down guys in the right-of-way appear to predate this project, so they may fall outside the scope of these requirements.\n\n**EL-1.43** - Transformers maintain adequate vertical clearance from overhead lines. The pad-mounted transformers shown on Sheet 21 are ground-level installations with no overhead lines positioned above them, indicating compliance.\n\n**EL-1.44** - Niche service configurations don't apply here since the transformers are pad-mounted rather than recessed installations.\n\n**EL-1.45** - Construction staging and equipment zones need verification for proper clearance from energized overhead lines. The site has overhead lines with construction activity planned nearby, but the Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan on Sheet 5 doesn't document the required 10-foot working clearance from these lines. **EL-1.46** - The plans lack a required notification requirement stating that the owner and contractor must alert Austin Energy at least 48 hours before commencing work near high-voltage overhead lines, which is necessary given the adjacent overhead infrastructure. elevations sheets. Additionally, there are no section or profile exhibits that show clearance dimensions between the conductors and buildings, and trees are proposed within 10 feet of the overhead electric infrastructure without documentation of underground compatibility.\n\nFor items that cannot be verified from the current plans, the lack of surveyed conductor elevations prevents confirmation of the required 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance, the 15-foot radial clearance, and building projection clearances. The plans also lack clearance verification from right-of-way facilities, fence clearance dimensions, fence post distances from poles, driveway vertical clearance data, and several other clearance-related specifications that would be needed to fully assess compliance. Looking back at EL-1.25, I need to reconsider whether this is actually unverifiable. The requirement is that sidewalks clear existing power poles at the property corners along Valley View Road. The site plan shows pole locations and proposed sidewalk layout, so I can visually assess whether they're positioned to avoid conflicts. This is something I should be able to determine from the available documentation rather than flagging as missing information.\n\nI should focus my findings on deficiencies that are genuinely undocumented or impossible to verify from what's provided, rather than items I haven't fully analyzed yet.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2823,"cache_read_input_tokens":75810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2823},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f67b20d3-b17d-4418-aa28-3e2e17c1cd9